ton=False).acquire(timeout=1)` on a thread. The call returned `Timeout` after 10.02 s, before the holder closed. The review did not establish what ends the wait at 10 s in the two-account runs; section `[1a]` of the same script, with the second account's handle, shows the same 10 s. In the same-account probe (`oplock_semantics_vm.py`) the open returned only when the holder closed, still running at 15 s. The probe also shows that the failed exclusive create and `os.utime` by path return at once, so the wait is in the read and the break, not in the create attempt.\n\n## Impact\n\nEach `acquire()` of a soft lock that finds a marker another account holds an oplock on runs past its `timeout`, `blocking=False` and `cancel_check` until the holder answers or closes, 10 s per attempt in the recorded two-account runs. A stale marker under that oplock is not broken, so no process acquires the lock while the holder keeps its handle. With `AsyncSoftFileLock(run_in_executor=False)` the wait is on the event loop thread and stops every task in the process; with the default executor one worker thread waits. The documentation names a soft lock as the option for network filesystems, after verification on the mount, and `FileLock` is a soft lock on builds without `fcntl` and after its `ENOSYS` fallback. Mutual exclusion is not affected and nothing other than the marker is read, written or deleted.\n\n## Workarounds\n\n- Keep soft-lock markers in a directory whose ACL grants other accounts no access to new files: a directory inside the user profile, or one with inheritance removed and only the owner, `SYSTEM` and `Administrators` granted (`icacls <dir> /inheritance:r /grant:r \"<owner>:(OI)(CI)F\" \"SYSTEM:(OI)(CI)F\" \"Administrators:(OI)(CI)F\"`). `mode` does not do this on Windows.\n- Run `AsyncSoftFileLock` with the default executor rather than `run_in_executor=False`, so that only a worker thread waits.\n- No `timeout`, `blocking` or `cancel_check` value bounds the wait.\n\n## Patched version\n\n4.2.1.\n\nFix: https://github.com/tox-dev/filelock/pull/835\n\n## Credits\n\nFound by the maintainers while reviewing the fix for the report Anthropic's OSS Scanner sent on 15 October 2026 (review round 4, finding F4).\n\n- 2026-10-15 23:08 UTC: report received (36 findings; this defect is not among them)\n- 2026-10-16 01:16 UTC: fix PR #835 opened, for the Linux lease finding of GHSA-7hmp-gf99-57hx\n- 2026-10-16 08:25 UTC: finding identified in the maintainers' review of PR #835\n- 2026-10-16 22:43 UTC: draft advisory created",
